<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Coppergate</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Coppergate is a street in the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, whose name comes from the Old Norse for street of the cup-makers. Excavations there by the York Archaeological Trust from 1976 to 1981 uncovered the waterlogged remains of houses, workshops and many thousands of objects from Viking-age Jorvik. They showed a crowded town of craftsmen and traders, and led to the opening of the Jorvik <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> Centre on the site in 1984.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/coppergate.jpg" alt="Coppergate" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gordon Hatton · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shops_in_Coppergate_-_geograph.org.uk_-_1155163.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
